{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "08-recursion.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Recursion and fixed points",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Write a recursive function without naming it: find the trick that lets a function call itself, and the combinator that packages it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The factorial is \"if $n = 0$ then $1$ else $n \\cdot \\mathsf{fact}\\ (n - 1)$\". The definition mentions $\\mathsf{fact}$, and a λ-term has no names to mention: `name := term` is shorthand, unfolded before reduction. How can a function refer to itself without a name?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The factorial needs lesson 7's predecessor (each lesson starts with only the library):",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "pred := λn. fst (n (λp. pair (snd p) (succ (snd p))) (pair 0 0))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake]\n> The natural first try is to write the name anyway. Inside its own definition `fact` is not yet defined, so it is a free variable: $\\mathsf{fact}\\ 0$ works, because it never recurses, and $\\mathsf{fact}\\ 1$ gets stuck on the free `fact`.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fact := λn. if (iszero n) 1 (mul n (fact (pred n)))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fact 0",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fact 1",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A term that copies itself",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "One term from lesson 4 does refer to itself, in a way: $\\omega = \\lambda x.\\, x\\ x$, applied to itself, hands itself its own copy.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "normal 2: omega omega",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "That is the trick. A function cannot name itself, but it can be **given** itself, as an argument. Add a parameter $\\mathit{self}$, write $\\mathit{self}\\ \\mathit{self}$ where the function would call itself, and start it off as $\\omega$ does, by applying it to itself:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "G := λself. λn. if (iszero n) 1 (mul n (self self (pred n)))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "G G 3",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$G\\ G\\ 3$ becomes \"if $3 = 0$ then $1$ else $3 \\cdot (G\\ G\\ (\\mathsf{pred}\\ 3))$\": the copy of $G$ it was given is passed on, with a copy of itself.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Packaging the trick: Y",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Writing $\\mathit{self}\\ \\mathit{self}$ in every recursive function is a nuisance. We would rather write the honest step, $F = \\lambda \\mathit{self}.\\, \\lambda n.\\, \\ldots\\ \\mathit{self}\\ (n - 1)$, and have something tie the knot. We want a term $X$ with $F\\ X = X$: then $X$ is the factorial, since putting it in for $\\mathit{self}$ gives it back. $X$ is a **fixed point** of $F$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> $\\omega\\ \\omega$ steps to itself. Slip an $F$ into $\\omega$ so that each copy is passed through $F$: what does $(\\lambda x.\\, F\\ (x\\ x))\\ (\\lambda x.\\, F\\ (x\\ x))$ step to?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "It steps to $F\\ ((\\lambda x.\\, F\\ (x\\ x))\\ (\\lambda x.\\, F\\ (x\\ x)))$: $F$ applied to the term we started from. A fixed point of $F$. Abstract over $F$:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] The Y combinator\n> $Y = \\lambda f.\\, (\\lambda x.\\, f\\ (x\\ x))\\ (\\lambda x.\\, f\\ (x\\ x))$ satisfies $Y\\ g = g\\ (Y\\ g)$ for every $g$: $Y\\ g$ is a fixed point of $g$. The equation is up to β: the two sides reduce to a common term, not one to the other.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "normal 2: Y g",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "The last line is also what $g\\ (Y\\ g)$ reduces to, in one step. Each unfolding hands $g$ another copy of the self-application, as many as it asks for.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Factorial",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$Y\\ F$ has no normal form (it unfolds for ever), so the engine keeps the definition as written; applied to a number, $F$'s test stops the unfolding. Defining `fact` again replaces the stuck one:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fact := Y (λself. λn. if (iszero n) 1 (mul n (self (pred n))))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fact 2",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fact 3",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "That took 1030 β-steps, and $\\mathsf{fact}\\ 4$ takes 6732: numerals in unary, a predecessor that counts up from $0$ every time, and arguments copied unevaluated and computed again. The work shows the first steps and the last, and says how many it leaves out. This is why real languages build numbers in.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Under call by value",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> $Y\\ g$ reduces to $g\\ (A\\ A)$ with $A = \\lambda x.\\, g\\ (x\\ x)$. Call by value evaluates an argument before the call. What happens to $A\\ A$?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$A\\ A$ steps to $g\\ (A\\ A)$, whose argument is $A\\ A$ again: call by value never finishes evaluating $g$'s argument, so $g$ is never called.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cbv 3: Y g",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The repair is to make the self-application a value, which waits until it is called: wrap it in a λ, $\\lambda v.\\, x\\ x\\ v$ (an η-expansion, lesson 4).",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] The Z combinator\n> $Z = \\lambda f.\\, (\\lambda x.\\, f\\ (\\lambda v.\\, x\\ x\\ v))\\ (\\lambda x.\\, f\\ (\\lambda v.\\, x\\ x\\ v))$. $Z\\ g$ reduces to $g$ applied to a λ that wraps the self-application: a value, so call by value stops there, and the copy unfolds only when $g$ calls it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Z := λf. (λx. f (λv. x x v)) (λx. f (λv. x x v))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cbv: Z g",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Turing completeness\n> With booleans, numerals, pairs and a fixed-point combinator, every computable function on the numbers can be written as a λ-term (Kleene; Turing showed λ-definable and Turing-computable coincide). Which terms have a normal form is then undecidable, which is why the engine reduces on a budget.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lean has no Y: self-application has no type (λ-calculus II shows why), and a recursive Lean definition must be shown to terminate, as a recursion on a smaller number is. Yet the factorial is still a fixed point of its defining step.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "def fact : Nat → Nat\n  | 0 => 1\n  | n + 1 => (n + 1) * fact n\n\n#eval fact 5\n\n/-- The step that defines the factorial, with the recursive call abstracted. -/\ndef F (self : Nat → Nat) : Nat → Nat\n  | 0 => 1\n  | n + 1 => (n + 1) * self n\n\ntheorem fact_fixed : ∀ n, F fact n = fact n := by\n  intro n; cases n <;> rfl",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem fact_pos : ∀ n, 0 < fact n := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "The factorial is never zero. Prove it by induction.",
      "leanSolution": "  intro n\n  induction n with\n  | zero => decide\n  | succ k ih => simp [fact, ih]",
      "hints": [
        "`induction n with`: `fact 0 = 1`, and `(k + 1) * fact k` is positive when `fact k` is.",
        "`simp [fact, ih]` knows a product of positives is positive."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "normal 1: Y g",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Take one step of $Y\\ g$ in normal order. What is the term?",
      "hints": [
        "Put $g$ for $f$ in $Y$'s body."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fact 1",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $\\mathsf{fact}\\ 1$.",
      "hints": [
        "$1 \\cdot \\mathsf{fact}\\ 0 = 1$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Y (λself. λn. if (iszero n) 0 (add n (self (pred n)))) 3",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Change the factorial so that it sums instead: $0$ at $0$, and $n$ plus the sum up to $n - 1$ otherwise. Write it with $Y$ in a cell of your own. What does it give for $3$?",
      "hints": [
        "Replace `1` by `0` and `mul` by `add` in the step given to `Y`.",
        "$3 + 2 + 1 + 0$."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> A function cannot name itself, but it can be given itself: self-application, as in $\\omega$. $Y$ packages the trick: $Y\\ g = g\\ (Y\\ g)$, a fixed point. Call by value needs $Z$, which delays the self-application. With fixed points the λ-calculus computes everything a computer can, and so whether a term stops is undecidable.\n\nλ-calculus II adds types, and finds that they rule out $\\omega$, $\\Omega$ and $Y$: every simply typed term has a normal form.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
